UNCTAD's Automated System for Customs Data (ASYCUDA) is an integrated customs management system for international trade and transport operations, providing technical assistance and capacity building for comprehensive Customs modernization.

Through the ASYCUDA programme, UNCTAD to:
Modernizing customs operations and helping to improve revenue collection;
Facilitating trade efficiency and competitiveness by substantially reducing transaction time and costs;
Improving security by streamlining procedures of cargo control, transit of goods and clearance of goods;
Helping fight corruption by enhancing the transparency of transactions;
Promoting sustainable development by cutting down on the use of paper, through the use of electronic transactions and documents

The project purpose is to provide technical assistance to Trinidad and Tobago (T&T) in the design of a comprehensive National Trade Strategy (NTS). 
The NTS seeks to bring about a concerted national vision on how to enhance T&T's trade competitiveness, particularly for SMEs, and a coherent strategic framework for trade development. Outcomes also include the building of capacities among national stakeholders to design and update the National Trade Strategy in the future.
The ultimate goal is to mainstream trade in T&T's economic planning, with a view to diversifying the economy, strengthening SMEs competitiveness and trade/investment support institutions, and focusing on priority sectors and markets.
Broadly, the NTS will provide:
A comprehensive strategic framework for export development, economic diversification, and SME competitiveness, including up to 6 sector strategies and up to 4 trade-support-function strategies (the total number of strategies will equal 10 but the number of sector and function strategies will depend on needs);
An informed policy framework to support development and competitiveness of the export sector with a positive human development impact;
More effective, and increased capacity of private and public institutions to support and stimulate exports through the provision of relevant and accessible trade support services;
A framework for more competitive SME capable of connecting to national, regional and global value-chains.

Support will be provided to a wide range of civil society organisations to be more meaningfully involved in the national HIV response. In this regard, support will be provided to 
(1) Establish the alliance of CSOs-CHANTT; 
(2) Conduct advocacy based on research and analysis on the risk and vulnerabilities of mothers living with HIV, including a policy review of healthcare protocols and their alignment with latest available evidence, and identification of gaps for policy review, reform, sensitization and capacity building of community-led organizations of women living with HIV; 
(3) Support to conduct anti-discrimination strategy planning sessions for decision makers, gov officials, NGOs, CSOs, FBOs  in adovacy and policy in Human Right challenges affecting the people living with and at risk of HIV;contributing from their respective agencies a strategic plan to address S&D   (Sustainability); 
(4) Capacity building support to CSOs to strengthen governance and improve ability to receive grants and participate meaningfully in the national HIV response; and 
(5) Biannual HIV EEHR Partner Forum to foster joint actions and support Civil Society sustainability in the country’s response to HIV and AIDS, including for addressing stigma and discrimination and promoting human rights.

